---
import Adr from "../../components/Adr.astro";
import Layer from "../../components/Layer.astro";
import Layout from "../../components/Layout.astro";
import PageLink from "../../components/PageLink.astro";
import Path from "../../components/Path.astro";
import Rule from "../../components/Rule.astro";

const ST = "server/src/iki_server/entities/states.py";
const S = "server/src/iki_server/entities/services.py";

const toc = [
  { id: "short", label: "A lock is held by the database" },
  { id: "how", label: "Locking, correcting and unlocking" },
  { id: "rules", label: "The rules in full" },
  { id: "ref", label: "Files and decisions" },
];
---

<Layout toc={toc}>
  <Layer n={1} title="A lock is held by the database">
    <p>
      While any of a record's state fields is in a state with <code>locks: true</code>, the record is
      locked: its other values can't change by any path, and it can't be archived, restored or moved
      to another registry entry. The services refuse first, saying which states would unlock it and
      who sets them; a trigger on every table holding the record's values refuses too, whatever
      writes. A locked record changes again only once a state that doesn't lock is set. An unsigned
      lock can name an <code>on_edit</code> state, so a reasoned edit returns it there and changes the
      values in one transaction. Once a record has been locked, every later edit of its values needs
      a reason (<Adr n={[120, 125, 179]} />).
    </p>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={2} title="Locking, correcting and unlocking">
    <p>
      <strong>Locking.</strong> Setting a locking state puts its field in <code
        >Entity.locked_by</code
      >. Setting a state clears <code>locked_by</code> for its own write, under the row lock, then
      sets it again from the new states (<Path p={ST} sym="def write(" />). A trigger keeps <code
        >has_been_locked</code
      > true once <code>locked_by</code> has held anything, across reopening and plugin migrations
      (<Path p="server/src/iki_server/entities/migrations/0024_remember_record_lock.py" />).
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>The database's guard.</strong> <code>sync_plugins</code> installs a <code
        >BEFORE INSERT OR UPDATE OR DELETE</code
      > trigger, <code>iki_locked__&lt;table&gt;</code>, on every type and list table of a type with
      state fields (<Path p={ST} sym="def install_guards(" />). It calls <code>iki_locked_guard</code>,
      which raises <code>check_violation</code> when the record's <code>locked_by</code> isn't empty
      (<Path p="server/src/iki_server/entities/migrations/0012_states.py" sym="iki_locked_guard" />).
      A plugin's migration that must write a locked record's tables, to move records out of a
      removed state, retire a state field or convert a quantity's unit, sets <code>iki.unlocked</code>{" "}
      for its own transaction (<Path
        p="server/src/iki_server/plugins/operations.py"
        sym="def unlocked("
      />). A pipeline that recomputes values has to reopen locked records first, as a person would.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Correcting under an unsigned lock.</strong> Editing a locked record is refused unless
      every locking state it's in names an <code>on_edit</code> state and isn't one that was signed
      for. Then <code>PATCH /api/v1/entities/{"{ref}"}</code>, with a reason, first returns each field
      to its <code>on_edit</code> state, with the full role and guard checks, in declaration order,
      then changes the values: two revisions or more, in one transaction (<Path
        p={ST}
        sym="def return_for_edit("
      />, <Path p={S} sym="allow_correction" />). A failed check or a stale revision rolls back the
      whole save. Only the PATCH route opts in; runs and imports never correct. The record's <code
        >can.correct</code
      > tells the app whether this person can (<Path p="server/src/iki_server/api/entities.py" sym="def patch_entity(" />).
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Reopening a signed lock.</strong> A state entered by signing isn't corrected on edit. An
      authorized signer reopens it with a signed reversal and a reason, and then the editor's own
      reason goes on each value revision (<PageLink to="Electronic signatures" hash="reversals" />).
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Every edit after an unlock needs a reason.</strong> <Path p={S} sym="def _revise(" />{" "}
      refuses an <code>edited</code> revision without a reason on a record that has been locked. A
      run that changes such a record needs its launcher's reason before it's queued, and each revision
      it writes carries it (<Path p="server/src/iki_server/runs/services.py" />). The record's <code
        >can.edit_reason_required</code
      > tells the app to ask (<Adr n="0126" />).
    </p>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={4} title="The rules in full">
    <div class="rules">
      <Rule title="What a refusal says" open>
        <p>
          <Path p={ST} sym="def locked_why(" /> names the fields that lock it and the states, with
          their roles, that would unlock it: "SAM-000012 is locked by its Status (Approved): it
          can’t be changed until its Status is set to Registered (Coordinator), … or In review
          (Analyst)" (<Adr n="0125" />). Archiving, restoring and moving
          to a superseding entry need every lock lifted first.
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Guards can read what was edited">
        <p>
          <code>edits.&lt;state field&gt;.&lt;state&gt;</code> lists the value edits since the record
          last entered that state, each with its editor and the fields it changed. Returning to a
          state starts a fresh list. So a reviewer who corrected a value can be kept from approving
          it: <code>edits.review.draft.all(e, e.by != actor.person)</code> (<Adr n="0179" />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="A released result is final" id="final">
        <p>
          A result released to the project that requested it after signing for its reviewed
          revision keeps that revision in <code>released_review_revision</code>. From then its values
          and states can't change: <code>Entity.locked</code> is true for it whatever its states (<Adr
            n="0149"
          />, <Path p={ST} sym="def _final_release(" />). <PageLink to="Release and completion" />{" "}
          covers how a result gets there.
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Where the ADRs and the code differ">
        <ul>
          <li><Adr n="0120" /> names <code>iki_entity.locked</code>; the column is the <code>locked_by</code> array, and <code>locked</code> is a property of it.</li>
        </ul>
      </Rule>
    </div>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={5} title="Files and decisions">
    <div class="refs">
      <div>
        <h3>Where</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><Path p={ST} /></li>
          <li><Path p={S} sym="def update(" /></li>
          <li><Path p="server/src/iki_server/entities/migrations/0012_states.py" /></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Decisions</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><Adr n="0120" title /></li>
          <li><Adr n="0125" title /></li>
          <li><Adr n="0149" title /></li>
          <li><Adr n="0179" title /></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Layer>
</Layout>
